 statement is justified?","correct":"Study A provides stronger evidence against H₀ than Study B.","distractors":["Study A proves H₀ is false while Study B proves H₀ is true.","Study B has a larger effect size because its p-value is larger.","The p-values show that Study A's population parameter is 0.004."],"e":"Smaller p-values indicate data less compatible with the null model, so Study A provides stronger evidence against H₀. P-values do not by themselves prove hypotheses or measure effect size."},{"unit":"U3","topicCode":"3.7","skill":"3.E","q":"In a test of H₀: p = 0.50, a sample of n = 200 has p̂ = 0.57. What is the z test statistic?","correct":"About 1.98","distractors":["About 0.14","About 2.80","About 4.04"],"e":"Under H₀, SE = √[0.50(0.50)/200] ≈ 0.03536. Thus z = (0.57−0.50)/0.03536 ≈ 1.98. The positive statistic reflects that the observed sample proportion is above the null value."},{"unit":"U3","topicCode":"3.7","skill":"4.G","q":"A test of H₀: p = 0.30 versus Hₐ: p > 0.30 gives p-value 0.032 at α = 0.05. What conclusion is appropriate?","correct":"Reject H₀; there is convincing statistical evidence that the population proportion exceeds 0.30.","distractors":["Fail to reject H₀ because 0.032 is larger than the null proportion 0.30.","Accept H₀ and conclude the population proportion equals 0.30.","Reject H₀ and conclude every individual in the population has probability above 0.30."],"e":"Because 0.032 < 0.05, the result is statistically significant and H₀ is rejected. The conclusion should be stated as evidence about the population proportion, not as certainty."},{"unit":"U3","topicCode":"3.8","skill":"2.D","q":"For H₀: p = 0.60 versus Hₐ: p > 0.60, what is a Type I error?","correct":"Concluding that p > 0.60 when in fact p = 0.60","distractors":["Failing to conclude p > 0.60 when in fact p > 0.60","Concluding p = 0.60 after the null hypothesis has been proven","Using a sample proportion different from 0.60"],"e":"A Type I error is rejecting a true null hypothesis. Here that means claiming the population proportion exceeds 0.60 when the null value 0.60 is actually correct."},{"unit":"U3","topicCode":"3.8","skill":"2.D","q":"For a fixed alternative value, which change generally increases the power of a significance test without changing the significance level?","correct":"Increase the sample size.","distractors":["Decrease the sample size.","Make the null and alternative parameter values closer together.","Increase sampling variability while keeping n fixed."],"e":"A larger sample size reduces standard error, making a real departure from H₀ easier to detect. Power also tends to increase for larger effect sizes, while added variability reduces power."},{"unit":"U3","topicCode":"3.9","skill":"3.D","q":"Independent populations have p₁ = 0.40 and p₂ = 0.25. Samples of sizes n₁ = 100 and n₂ = 150 are taken. What is the mean of the sampling distribution of p̂₁ − p̂₂?","correct":"0.15","distractors":["0 under an equal-proportions null","(0.40+0.25)/2=0.325","0.40+0.25=0.65"],"e":"The expected value of a difference in sample proportions is the difference in population proportions: E(p̂₁−p̂₂)=p₁−p₂=0.40−0.25=0.15."},{"unit":"U3","topicCode":"3.9","skill":"3.D","q":"For independent samples, which expression is the standard deviation of p̂₁ − p̂₂ when population proportions p₁ and p₂ are known?","correct":"√[p₁(1−p₁)/n₁ + p₂(1−p₂)/n₂]","distractors":["√[(p₁−p₂)(1−p₁+p₂)/(n₁+n₂)]","p₁(1−p₁)/n₁ + p₂(1−p₂)/n₂","√[p₁(1−p₁)/n₁] − √[p₂(1−p₂)/n₂]"],"e":"For independent sample proportions, variances add when taking a difference. Therefore the variance is the sum of the two component variances and the standard deviation is the square root of that sum."},{"unit":"U3","topicCode":"3.10","skill":"2.C","q":"Two independent random samples estimate the difference p₁ − p₂ between population proportions. Which interval procedure is appropriate?","correct":"A two-sample z-interval for p₁ − p₂","distractors":["A paired t-interval for a mean difference","A one-sample z-interval for a single proportion","A chi-square goodness-of-fit interval"],"e":"The target is the difference between two population proportions based on independent samples, so the appropriate method is a two-sample z-interval for p₁−p₂."},{"unit":"U3","topicCode":"3.10","skill":"3.E","q":"Sample 1 has p̂₁ = 0.62 with n₁ = 200, and Sample 2 has p̂₂ = 0.54 with n₂ = 180. What is the point estimate for p₁ − p₂?","correct":"0.08","distractors":["(0.62+0.54)/2=0.58","0.54−0.62